# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GATE-1 EVALUATOR-BUNDLE CLOSURE -- git's own identity machinery, pinned
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# daedalus/ignition/bundle.py identifies each evaluator module by GIT'S OWN
# blob sha (`git hash-object`), not raw file bytes, and that mechanism is
# ALREADY checkout-stable without a -text pin: `git hash-object` reproduces
# the same normalisation `git add` would, so an LF commit and a CRLF working
# copy of it hash to the same blob sha (MEASURED,
# tests/test_ignition_bundle.py::test_the_digest_does_not_move_with_line_endings).
# This block closes a narrower, real gap beside that one: `evaluator_bundle`
# also records `running_bytes_sha256` -- the RAW bytes that actually
# executed -- and marks it platform_dependent because on this host
# (core.autocrlf=true) it differs from the committed LF bytes; the same
# mismatch was exactly the Gate-1 fixture's CRLF defect above. `-text` makes
# the working-tree bytes equal the committed bytes on every host, so the
# bytes that ran and the bytes anyone can fetch stop merely CORRESPONDING
# (matched via a git blob sha both sides recompute) and become the SAME
# bytes -- and it removes the evaluator closure's one remaining dependency on
# git's implicit text/binary detection for a package a Gate-1 verdict
# entirely depends on.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-24 (`git check-attr text --stdin` over
# `daedalus.ignition.bundle.import_closure(ROOT, EVALUATOR_MODULES)`, 124
# modules, every one under daedalus/): 123 of 124 resolved `text: unspecified`
# -- no explicit, non-autocrlf-dependent declaration at all -- the one
# exception (daedalus/runtimes/provider_observation.py) already pinned above
# for an unrelated reason. And a SECOND measurement the first draft of this
# section got wrong: checking raw file bytes (not `git ls-files --eol`'s
# INDEX column, which is always `i/lf`) showed 121 of these 124 files are
# CRLF ON DISK right now, working-tree side, in this worktree -- the "947
# tracked files are CRLF" the D7 section above already measured, just not
# cross-checked against this specific closure before now.
#
# AN EXPLICIT LIST, NOT A WILDCARD, ON PURPOSE -- the opposite call from the
# first draft of this section. `daedalus/**/*.py -text` looked like the
# self-maintaining choice (closure grows with every refactor, per bundle.py's
# own docstring), but MEASURED against this worktree it would silently
# -text-pin roughly 170 OTHER daedalus modules the Gate-1 bundle never reads,
# most of them ALSO CRLF-on-disk over an LF-committed blob -- exactly the
# "whole-repository EOL policy" the D7 section above declined to make for
# `*.py`, just scoped to one directory instead of the repository. `git status`
# does not surface this today only because those files' mtimes have not
# changed since git's index last cached them clean; the first `git add` (or
# `git add -A`) that touches one after this file starts asking `-text`'s
# question would silently flip an unrelated module's committed line endings.
# The list below is exactly the 124 the bundle's closure computes -- nothing
# more -- and every one of them has ALSO been renormalised to LF in this
# worktree (a working-tree-only change; no committed byte moves, matching
# every -text pin above), so `-text` here changes nothing about to be
# committed. tests/test_ignition_bundle_gitattributes.py re-derives the
# closure from source on every run via `git check-attr` and fails on any gap,
# so a closure that outgrows this list is a red test, not a silent hole --
# the same self-healing property a wildcard would have given for free, without
# the blast radius a wildcard measured to carry here.
